" (https://files.peacecorps.gov/documents/ED204_Setting-Up-and-Running-a-School-Library.pdf)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 146; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view0\",\"lineno\":1018}); Total lines: 5122\nL867@P29: • coloured marker pens (indelible ink, waterproof pens are best.\nL868@P29: Felt pens are acceptable)\nL869@P29: • drawing pins\nL870@P29: • envelopes\nL871@P29: • glue (rubber-based glues, like Copydex, are good, so are glue sticks\nL872@P29: -buy both if you can). There are many types of glue for sale in\nL873@P29: shops but some are not suitable for use by young children. Spray\nL874@P29: glue and extra-strong 'super' glues should be avoided.\nL875@P29: • ink pad refills\nL876@P29: • masking tape or coloured sticky 'electrical' tape for book repairs\nL877@P29: and/or classification\nL878@P29: • ledger for accession register\nL879@P29: • several exercise books, including one for accounts, one for book\nL880@P29: or magazine orders, one for borrowing records and one for\nL881@P29: queries and/or suggestions\nL882@P29: • paper clips\nL883@P29: • pencils\nL884@P29: • staples\nL885@P29: • sticky tape, dots or labels (plain ones can be coloured with pens\nL886@P29: if you choose a colour coded classification system). Primary\nL887@P29: school pupils and their teachers might find it helpful to use\nL888@P29: colours - for example red, yellow and blue -to divide books into\nL889@P29: skill levels. This can help young children to select books from the\nL890@P29: library stock.\nL891@P29: • cut and ruled record cards (sometimes called index cards or guide\nL892@P29: cards). These are sold in packets in a variety of standard sizes.\nL893@P29: The recommended size is 125 x 76 mm (5 x 3 inches) but as\nL894@P29: long as they fit the record or issue box it does not matter what\nL895@P29: size they are. If you find these cards hard to obtain you could\nL896@P29: make your own. You could also try asking printers for paper\nL897@P29: offcuts if you have very little money to make library purchases.\nL898@P29: To make your own record cards cut up stiff paper. Make divider\nL899@P29: cards of 125 x 85 mm (5 x 3I/2 inches) in the same way, but from\nL900@P29: coloured card.\nL901@P29: • paper.\nL902@P29: Optional stationery\nL903@P29: • stencils (for making neat posters and shelf guides)\nL904@P29-30: • typewriter ribbon • Sellotape can be useful, but it tends to dry, shrink and fall off.\nL905@P30: For small book repairs you could use glue or clear sticky tape like\nL906@P30: Scotch 3M Magic Tape.\nL907@P30: Teaching\nL908@P30: tips\nL909@P30: 2\nL910@P30: A bookcase with five shelves of 180 x 90 em (6 x 3\nL911@P30: feet) will hold about 15 0 books. If your library has\nL912@P30: this number of books and only one bookcase there\nL913@P30: may be a problem when students come into the\nL914@P30: library. They will find it difficult to reach the books on\nL915@P30: the top shelves and there will be such a crowd that it\nL916@P30: will be hard for students to find the books they are\nL917@P30: looking for. In this case it is best to have several/ower\nL918@P30: shelves arranged around the library room, rather than\nL919@P30: just one bookcase.\nL920@P30: You could ask the woodwork teacher to help you\nL921@P30: make the library and its furniture. Remind him or her\nL922@P30: to protect the shelves against warping and insects. If\nL923@P30: possible, try to make some shelves adjustable (see\nL924@P30: figure 3.6), so that larger books can be arranged on\nL925@P30: them too.\nL926@P30: 3 Ask students to make two issue boxes in which you\nL927@P30: can file the title cards and, depending on your lending\nL928@P30: method, borrowers' cards. A good alternative is to use\nL929@P30: clean shoeboxes. Some librarians make a small hole\nL930@P30: at the bottom of all index cards and then put a thin\nL931@P30: stick through all the cards to prevent their alpha\u0002betical order being accidentally confused. This device\nL932@P30: saves a lot of time if the issue box is ever dropped.\nL933@P30: How to keep simple accounts\nL934@P30: It is essential to keep a record of everything you spend on the\nL935@P30: library room, furniture, equipment, stationery and stock. This will\nL936@P30: involve two main tasks.\nL937@P30: 1 Always ask for a receipt (a note of the cost of your purchases\nL938@P30: and the date, written on headed paper with the seller's\nL939@P30: signature) when you buy anything for the library. Keep all\nL940@P30: receipts in a safe place. At least once a year the school\nL941@P30: accountant is sure to need to see these receipts.\nL942@P30: 2 As soon as you have bought any item write down the date, the\nL943@P30: amount spent and a short description of what you have bought\nL944@P30: /\nL945@P30-31: 25 26\nL946@P31: in your accounts notebook. Your notebook should be ruled\nL947@P31: into four columns headed Date, What bought?, Price and\nL948@P31: Total.\nL949@P31: Why keep accounts?\nL950@P31: Keeping account records helps you check how much money you\nL951@P31: have spent on the library. It will also help you budget how much\nL952@P31: money you need to spend on the library next year. The school\nL953@P31: accountant will also need to see your records.\nL954@P31-32: ' 4 Getting to know your\nL955@P32: library stock\nL956@P32: ••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••\nL957@P32: What stock is in your library?\nL958@P32: When you organise your library, it is important to get to know the\nL959@P32: range and type of stock you have and to divide your stock into\nL960@P32: three basic sections - reference, information and fiction.\nL961@P32: Reference material\nL962@P32: This includes encyclopaedias, dictionaries and atlases. Reference\nL963@P32: books are used to find quick answers, check facts, answer students'\nL964@P32: questions and discover new information. Because reference books\nL965@P32: are often large, expensive and in constant demand, most libraries\nL966@P32: do not lend them. Instead librarians put them on clearly labelled\nL967@P32: reference bookshelves next to the information books.\nL968@P32: Information material\nL969@P32: This is sometimes known as non-fiction and is stock about people,\nL970@P32: places and things. Most of the stock in your library, including\nL971@P32: textbooks, will belong to this group. To help library users find the\nL972@P32: book they want, it is recommended that the teacher-librarian\nL973@P32: organises all the information stock into different subject areas. For\nL974@P32: example, put all the books on trees and forests on bookshelves\nL975@P32: close to each other. You will read more about how to do this in\nL976@P32: chapter 7.\nL977@P32: Fiction\nL978@P32: These books are also known as story books or novels. Some fiction\nL979@P32: books are used by English teachers in secondary schools all round\nL980@P32: the world; Animal Farm by George Orwell and Things Fall Apart by\nL981@P32: Chinua Achebe are two examples. Fiction books are not just for\nL982@P32: studying, they can also be read for fun: Tales Of The Tikongs by\nL983@P32: Epeli Hau'opa is a good example of a novel that students will\nL984@P32: enjoy.\nL985@P32: Once you have divided the stock into reference, information and\nL986@P32: fiction sections, it will be clear to you that the stock comes in\nL987@P32: many different shapes and forms - including books, pamphlets,\nL988@P32: booklets, magazines, newspapers, flipcharts, posters, photo albums\nL989@P32: of school forms, maps, videos, audio cassettes, etc. The most\nL990@P32: important type of stock, however, will be the books.\nL991@P32-33: 27 28\nL992@P33: More about books\nL993@P33: Books are made of paper and so they can be easily damaged. It is\nL994@P33: just as important to show students how to treat books well as it is\nL995@P33: to encourage them to make more use of the books in the library.\nL996@P33: One way to do both is to make sure that students and library\nL997@P33: monitors know all about what a book is and are able to name the\nL998@P33: different parts of a book.\nL999@P33: How to help students learn about the parts of a book\nL1000@P33: The best method is to give a form lesson. Give each student a\nL1001@P33: book. Then point out the different parts of a book and explain\nL1002@P33: their purpose. Ask students to find the same parts on their own\nL1003@P33: book. Remember to explain the uses of the contents and index\nL1004@P33: pages as these can help students with their studies. Use figures 4.1\nL1005@P33: and 4.2 and the information below. For more ideas about library\nL1006@P33: lessons see chapters 13 and 14.\nL1007@P33: Back cover\nL1008@P33: Title and\nL1009@P33: author's name\nL1010@P33: are printed on\nL1011@P33: the book spine.\nL1012@P33: Look for the\nL1013@P33: ISBN and bar\nL1014@P33: code on the\nL1015@P33: back cover.\nL1016@P33: Top of book\nL1017@P33: Bottom of book\nL1018@P33: Figure 4.1 Learning about the parts of a book\nL1019@P33: The parts of a book\nL1020@P33: Publisher\nL1021@P33: Title on the\nL1022@P33: front cover\nL1023@P33: Front cover\nL1024@P33: Author's name\nL1025@P33: on front cover\nL1026@P33: • Cover This can be hard (hardback) or soft (paperback). The\nL1027@P33: cover helps protect the book. Some teacher-librarians like to put\nL1028@P33: an extra cover, which they buy from library suppliers or make\nL1029@P33: from strong paper or sticky-back plastic, on popular books.\nL1030@P33: • Front cover This may have a picture on it and usually has the\nL1031@P33: title of the book and the author's name; it may also have the\nL1032@P33-34: publisher's name. • Back cover This often has a summary of what the book is\nL1033@P34: about; or it may have some people's comments about how much\nL1034@P34: they enjoyed the book- these are called reviews. Most back\nL1035@P34: covers list the book's own internationally recognised ten-digit\nL1036@P34: ISBN, usually nowadays in the form of a computer readable bar\nL1037@P34: code. An ISBN is a computer number used by publishers and\nL1038@P34: booksellers to identify a title. In some large bookshops you will\nL1039@P34: be able to order the books you want if you give the bookseller\nL1040@P34: just this number. However, it is not essential information for\nL1041@P34: libraries without computers.\nL1042@P34: • Spine This is the backbone of a book. If it breaks, or is\nL1043@P34: damaged, there is a risk that the book's pages will fall out. On\nL1044@P34: the spine most books usually have the title of the book and the\nL1045@P34: author as well as a symbol (picture or letters) which identifies the\nL1046@P34: publisher.\nL1047@P34: • Spine label All the books in your library should have a spine\nL1048@P34: label, glued or stuck to the bottom of the spine. This will be used\nL1049@P34: by students to locate books they want and to find out if the book\nL1050@P34: is information or fiction; it will also give a visual reminder of\nL1051@P34: what the book is about. If, for example, a student is looking for a\nL1052@P34: biology book, he or she (depending on your classification\nL1053@P34: system) would look for green spine labels or the classification\nL1054@P34: code number 500. (See chapter 7 for more information about\nL1055@P34: classification systems.)\nL1056@P34: • Title page This is usually a right-hand page near the front of\nL1057@P34: the book. On it will be the book's title, author and publisher.\nL1058@P34: • Title verso page This is traditionally the left-hand page\nL1059@P34: immediately after the title page. On it will be more information\nL1060@P34: about the publisher (for example, the publisher's address) and\nL1061@P34: about the book (what year it was published, if it has been\nL1062@P34: reprinted and who printed it). The title verso page will also have\nL1063@P34: details of the book's copyright.\nL1064@P34: • School nameplate and return date label It is recommended\nL1065@P34: that you glue your school nameplate and a return date label on\nL1066@P34: to the first right-hand page as you open the book (see figure 4.3).\nL1067@P34: Explain to students that this shows who owns the book and that\nL1068@P34: it is also the place to find out what day they should return the\nL1069@P34: book if they have borrowed it from the library.\nL1070@P34-35: 29 30\nL1071@P35: Contents page\nL1072@P35: <\nL1073@P35: J ---\nL1074@P35: ... _--- - - <<\nL1075@P35: s_ -- _ - - 41\nL1076@P35: :r,.. .... , ............. so\nL1077@P35: de~~: ............. b.z\nL1078@P35: Title verso page\nL1079@P35: Look at the title verso page to find\nL1080@P35: out which company published the\nL1081@P35: book and when.\nL1082@P35: Put the return date label on the first page\nL1083@P35: after the inside front cover. If it has the\nL1084@P35: book's title and the author's name\nL1085@P35: printed on it, then this is the title page.\nL1086@P35: - '\"\nL1087@P35: Figure 4.2 Learning about the inside of a book\nL1088@P35: Inside front cover\nL1089@P35: Inside back cover\nL1090@P35: Look for the\nL1091@P35: index at the\nL1092@P35: back of\nL1093@P35: the book.\nL1094@P35: Pages\nL1095@P35: Title page\nL1096@P35: Title page with\nL1097@P35: return date label\nL1098@P35: stuck on top of it\nL1099@P35: Pages\nL1100@P35: Book's accession\nL1101@P35: number\nL1102@P35: Glue thin strip\nL1103@P35: School's name\nL1104@P35: - F\"T::J_Jil---- Columns to write\nL1105@P35: The school\nL1106@P35: nameplate is\nL1107@P35: folded up lj3 to\nL1108@P35: make a card\nL1109@P35: pocket.\nL1110@P35: or stamp the\nL1111@P35: date by which\nL1112@P35: the borrower\nL1113@P35: must return the\nL1114@P35: book to the\nL1115@P35: library\nL1116@P35-36: Figure 43 Where to place the return date label in a book • Contents page Most information books have a contents page.\nL1117@P36: This gives an outline, or sometimes a brief summary, of what will\nL1118@P36: be in each chapter- and a page number so that you can turn\nL1119@P36: straight to the right chapter. For example, in a book on the\nL1120@P36: lifecycle of a butterfly you might see from the contents page that\nL1121@P36: chapter 1 is on larvae, chapter 2 is on the chrysalis, chapter 3 is\nL1122@P36: on caterpillars and chapter 4 is on butterflies. If you want more\nL1123@P36: detailed information then you should look at both the contents\nL1124@P36: page and the index.\nL1125@P36: • Index An information book is more useful if it has an index.\nL1126@P36: This is usually an alphabetical list of subjects, people and other\nL1127@P36: important items that are written about in the book, each with a\nL1128@P36: page reference. An index is usually at the back of a book. The\nL1129@P36: page references make it easy for the user to find the information\nL1130@P36: they want. For example, use the index in this book to find out\nL1131@P36: where there is more information about different types of stock.\nL1132@P36: After the word 'stock', you will find several numbers. These\nL1133@P36: numbers direct you to the pages in the book which have\nL1134@P36: information about stock printed on them. Make sure your\nL1135@P36: students do not confuse an index with a glossary (or key words\nL1136@P36: section), which lists difficult or foreign words and explains what\nL1137@P36: they mean, but does not give page references.\nL1138@P36: Before students use the library, check that they know what is the\nL1139@P36: front cover, the back cover and the spine; how to tell the title from\nL1140@P36: the author (this can be quite difficult for students}; who the\nL1141@P36: publisher is; where to find the publication date and where to find\nL1142@P36: your own library details, like the return date label.\nL1143@P36: Teaching tip\nL1144@P36: Your students may be set a project that asks them one\nL1145@P36: question about how to grow rice, one question on how to\nL1146@P36: make aeroplanes and one question on Nelson Mandela's\nL1147@P36: work in South Africa.\nL1148@P36: Explain that if they want quick answers to these\nL1149@P36: questions they need to find a reference book and use the\nL1150@P36: index. In the index they should look for the best possible\nL1151@P36: word that describes the subject they want - (in this case\nL1152@P36: the words would be 'rice''aeroplanes' and 'Mandela?.\nL1153@P36: If they want more detailed information they should go to\nL1154@P36: the appropriate bookshelf in the library, and then check\nL1155@P36: the contents and index pages of relevant books.\nL1156@P36-37: 31 32\nL1157@P37: How to help students keep books in good condition\nL1158@P37: It is unfair to blame students for spoiling books if they have not\nL1159@P37: been taught how to look after them. It can be tempting to tell\nL1160@P37: students not to write inside a book; not to have dirty hands; not to\nL1161@P37: take books out in the rain; not to bend the corners of the page\nL1162@P37: when they forget their bookmarks; not to eat in the library; not to\nL1163@P37: pull the book's spine when they are taking a book off a shelf ..\nL1164@P37: but lists of 'Don't do this' and 'Don't do that' can discourage\nL1165@P37: students from using books. Instead explain to students that\nL1166@P37: because books are expensive, and can be easily damaged, they\nL1167@P37: must be treated with respect.\nL1168@P37: Simon Etherton trained 12 student library monitors including\nL1169@P37: Stephen Kapu and Nester Haiptu to help run the library at\nL1170@P37: Allardyce Secondary School, Solomon Islands. He suggests:\nL1171@P37: 'There is a lack of understanding about haw fragile books are. Students\nL1172@P37: need constant reminding about how to tum over pages and why they\nL1173@P37: should use paper bookmarks (rather than pens, rulers or even other\nL1174@P37: books).'\nL1175@P37: Using a library and its \\>.ooks requires skills. All school children will\nL1176@P37: need patient, repeated ~'Kplanations and a good example set by the\nL1177@P37: teacher-librarian and other staff. Remember also to make the\nL1178@P37: library a friendly place, otherwise students will not use it. \\.\nL1179@P37: More about other types of stock\nL1180@P37: Most libraries have a variety of stock besides books. This may\nL1181@P37: include magazines, newspapers, pamphlets and audio-visual stock.\nL1182@P37: The rest of this chapter looks at different types of stock that might\nL1183@P37: be good for your school library.\nL1184@P37: Newspapers\nL1185@P37: Newspapers can be published daily or weekly. They are often very\nL1186@P37: popular with students at school libraries, espedally if they are local\nL1187@P37: publications containing up-to-date news. However, old newspapers\nL1188@P37: soon turn yellow and attract dirt, insects and mice. A good idea is\nL1189@P37: to save back copies and then when you have a year's collection, to\nL1190@P37: arrange for them to be bound inside a strong hard-backed cover.\nL1191@P37: Daniel Aidoo, working as a library assistant at Central Regional\nL1192@P37: Library in Ghana, found:\nL1193@P37: 1\nL1194@P37: Bound volumes of periodicals [magazines] and newspapers are very\nL1195@P37: popular with students and are also helpful for reference purposes.'\nL1196@P37: If you decide to bind your newspapers it is recommended that you\nL1197@P37: let students read the issues for one or two weeks before you remove\nL1198@P37-38: J them from the library. Then you can store the newspapers in a dry\nL1199@P38: place, in a pile arranged in date order, ready for binding at the end\nL1200@P38: of the year.\nL1201@P38: An alternative is to remove newspapers from the library after three\nL1202@P38: months, and then to cut out the most interesting stories and\nL1203@P38: organise them into general sections in a subject file or project box\nL1204@P38: (seep. 36). In this way you could create subject files on, for example,\nL1205@P38: the Life of Ghandi; Leatherback turtles; the Aswan Dam; etc.\nL1206@P38: Another alternative is to do what Ruth Weitzel, working in the\nL1207@P38: Caribbean with librarians including Laurentia Israel, jacqueline\nL1208@P38: Mussington, Patricia Baptiste and Nellie Payne, suggests:\nL1209@P38: 'Rather than throw away unwanted issues of newspapers and\nL1210@P38: magazines, put them on a free table at the entrance to be given away.\nL1211@P38: Some students might appreciate the materials.'\nL1212@P38: Magazines\nL1213@P38: Magazines are publications produced on a regular basis, for\nL1214@P38: instance quarterly, monthly or weekly. They are often in colour\nL1215@P38: and have news items, feature stories, photos and advertisements\nL1216@P38: inside. A magazine can be on any subject: world events, soccer,\nL1217@P38: fashion, business, etc. Famous world-circulation magazines include\nL1218@P38: Time and Newsweek (both ~eport international events each week);\nL1219@P38: National Geographic (which,has excellent photographs of people\nL1220@P38: and the world); New Scientist (about science); The Economist (a\nL1221@P38: business magazine) and New Internationalist (facts about people's\nL1222@P38: lives around the world, and particularly developing countries).\nL1223@P38: The best magazines for secondary schools have plenty of pictures\nL1224@P38: that will raise students' curiosity. A carefully chosen display of\nL1225@P38: magazine pictures and picture captions on the wall can make\nL1226@P38: reluctant readers more enthusiastic about reading short news items.\nL1227@P38: Both magazines and newspapers can be displayed in browser boxes\nL1228@P38: or on special magazine racks. If you do not have these you could\nL1229@P38: make tidy piles of magazines so that students know where to find\nL1230@P38: their favourite newspapers or magazine. Each magazine should have\nL1231@P38: its own pile, with issues placed in date order- the newest on top.\nL1232@P38: You can also bind magazines - see the section on newspapers.\nL1233@P38: Figure 12.3 in chapter 12 shows how to make a strong magazine\nL1234@P38: folder.\nL1235@P38: Pamphlets\nL1236@P38: Pamphlets are small, thin books which contain information about\nL1237@P38-39: one subject. They are sometimes called booklets. They look 33 34\nL1238@P39: different from other books because they do not have a spine. This\nL1239@P39: means that if you arrange them on the bookshelves you cannot\nL1240@P39: read the title or the name of the author. One way to store\nL1241@P39: pamphlets is to put them in a project box. Because pamphlets are\nL1242@P39: cheaper (and quicker) to make than baoi<s, they often contain\nL1243@P39: useful, up-to-date information -for example health and nutrition\nL1244@P39: advice or advice about how to run a business.\nL1245@P39: Figure 4.4 shows the differences between a book, a pamphlet and a\nL1246@P39: magazine.\nL1247@P39: A pamphlet or\nL1248@P39: booklet is usually\nL1249@P39: thin. It is held\nL1250@P39: together by\nL1251@P39: staples, glue or\nL1252@P39: thread, and does\nL1253@P39: not have a spine.\nL1254@P39: Title of magazine\nL1255@P39: The paper cover\nL1256@P39: of a magazine\nL1257@P39: may be thick and\nL1258@P39: shiny. Price\nL1259@P39: A book has a\nL1260@P39: hard cover, or\nL1261@P39: paperback cover,\nL1262@P39: and an obvious\nL1263@P39: spine.\nL1264@P39: Magazines\nL1265@P39: always have a\nL1266@P39: paper cover.\nL1267@P39: Look for a date\nL1268@P39: and price on the\nL1269@P39: front cover. If\nL1270@P39: you find these,\nL1271@P39: you are probably\nL1272@P39: looking at a\nL1273@P39: magazine.\nL1274@P39-40: Figure 4.4 Differences between a book, a pamphlet and a magazine Flipcharts\nL1275@P40: Flipcharts are like teachers' form notes but they are drawn on\nL1276@P40: several pieces of poster-size paper. Flipcharts can save teachers a lot\nL1277@P40: of time, as they are a permanent record of diagrams that take\nL1278@P40: several minutes to draw on the chalkboard, only to be wiped out\nL1279@P40: by the next teacher.\nL1280@P40: Encourage teachers to make flipcharts (e.g., of the life cycle of a\nL1281@P40: frog for biology, or on how to vote in an election) for the subjects\nL1282@P40: they teach and then store them safely in the library. The best\nL1283@P40: method is to roll the flipcharts up, and label them clearly on the\nL1284@P40: outside. Make a cross-reference in the shelf guide and catalogue if\nL1285@P40: you have one. Then store the flipcharts in a project box, away\nL1286@P40: from dust.\nL1287@P40: Maps and posters\nL1288@P40: The best place for all maps and posters is pinned up on the library\nL1289@P40: walls or on formroom walls. Libraries look more interesting if you\nL1290@P40: display lots of colourful posters. If you are unable to display all\nL1291@P40: your posters, store them in a dust-free area- either folded in\nL1292@P40: project boxes, rolled up in cardboard poster tubes or in a cupboard.\nL1293@P40: Video cassettes, film reels and music tapes1\nL1294@P40: These are all audio-visual or audio equipmeht. They are usually\nL1295@P40: supplied in their own cases or boxes to protect them from dust. If\nL1296@P40: your school is in a humid climatic zone add a small packet of silica\nL1297@P40: gel to protect them. This works like a sponge absorbing the\nL1298@P40: moisture in the air. Silica gel is blue but as it absorbs moisture it\nL1299@P40: turns pinky purple, and then pink. Once it is pink it stops\nL1300@P40: absorbing moisture. You do not have to throw out silica gel when\nL1301@P40: you have used it. Just heat it up (over a low temperature) so that it\nL1302@P40: dries out and turns blue again, ready to be re-used. Silica gel can be\nL1303@P40: bought from chemists or pharmacy stores. If you have never seen\nL1304@P40: it before, ask people who are keen photographers to explain more\nL1305@P40: about how it works.\nL1306@P40: Audio-visual stock will include films and videos; audio stock will\nL1307@P40: include cassettes of music, oral (spoken) history and stories. Both\nL1308@P40: audio-visual and audio stock are useful because they allow students\nL1309@P40: to see things and hear sounds they may find hard to imagine. Used\nL1310@P40: well they can be excellent teaching tools. The problem is that you\nL1311@P40: will need electricity, or a generator, or batteries to use them. They\nL1312@P40: must also be stored carefully. If you have not yet used audio-visual\nL1313@P40: material for teaching purposes, and feel nervous about using it,\nL1314@P40: don't worry. Just experiment- the results are usually very good.\nL1315@P40: Morgan Armstrong, working at Pawa, an isolated, rural secondary\nL1316@P40-41: school in Solomon Islands, found that: 35 36\nL1317@P41: 'Every reading passage and book has to be selected carefully when new\nL1318@P41: words, ideas and concepts are introduced because students want to\nL1319@P41: know: \"What's a car, a light bulb or a crime?\"'\nL1320@P41: What would be a good ' way of showing students this information?\nL1321@P41: Take the example of a car. You could describe a car, draw a car on a\nL1322@P41: chalkboard or pass a picture of a car around the form. But a better\nL1323@P41: way might be to use audio-visual equipment to show a film about\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view1\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 1\n"

